IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `taslsl-dates`

-- Long Service Leave Act 1976 (Tasmania) -- the State's general long service leave Act: who it covers (ss 2, 3, 7),
-- mining employees (ss 2, 2A, Schedule 2), what counts as continuous employment (ss 2(2), 5), the entitlement (ss 7A, 8,
-- 8A), payment on death (s 9), payment in lieu by agreement (s 10), ordinary pay (s 11), how and when the leave is taken
-- and paid (s 12, including the workers' compensation rule of s 12(2)), disputes, recovery and prosecution (ss 13, 14A,
-- 15, 20, 21).
--
-- SEAT: the employee (or former employee) to whom the Act gives long service leave; for the questions a death raises
-- (s 9), the deceased employee's personal representative. "Does the Act cover me? Am I a mining employee? Did this break
-- keep my employment continuous? How many weeks am I entitled to? What must I be paid when my job ends? Can I be made to
-- take leave while I am on workers' compensation? Was I paid on time? Can I still recover it?" The employer, the
-- Secretary, the Commission and the magistrate get no seat: the Secretary's exemptions and directions (ss 7, 12(1)(a))
-- and every judgment ("justify", "serious and wilful misconduct", "reasonable grounds", "regularly employed") are asked as
-- facts, never decided.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list). The rule with a deadline
-- (s 12(4), (6)(a)) is a DEONTIC rule run inside one question; no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- Edition: the consolidated version published 2025-01-22, current on Tasmanian Legislation Online at 6 November 2026, as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/act-1976-095.txt. No regulations under the Act are deposited; the Act's
-- own figures do not depend on them.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: ss 1, 2A(2), 4, 19, 22, 24 (machinery); s 6 (employment before 1976 and before 1956); s 7(2)-(8) (the
-- Secretary's exemption procedure: asked as whether an exemption is in force); s 8(4) and s 8A(4) (employment that began
-- before the Long Service Leave Act 1964 or the 1980 proclaimed date; the dates are not in the deposited text); s 11(8),
-- (9) (stated in descriptions); s 12(7), (8) (adjustments and cheques); s 13(1A)-(4), s 14 (the dispute and appeal
-- procedure, in descriptions); s 16 (costs); ss 17, 18, 23 (in descriptions); ss 25, 26, 27 and Schedule 3 (transitional,
-- of historic reach). Reasons in NOTES.md.
--
-- Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (State of Tasmania, CC BY 4.0); an
-- unofficial plain-text conversion, see SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the employee's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- NEGATIVES. JUST FALSE where the text says no in its own words; NOTHING, with a NOTHING-kind tag, where it does not
-- answer (rulings of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05, copied in NOTES.md). Sites decided case by case carry a fork number
-- (NOTES.md, fork register).

§ `Long service leave in Tasmania: the employee's questions`

-- A yes or a no that the text gives in its own words.
GIVEN b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`yes or no` b MEANS IF b THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`not below zero` x MEANS IF x LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE x

-- s 8(2)(a)(i) "8 2/3 weeks' long service leave in respect of the first 10 years"; (ii) "4 1/3 weeks' ... in respect of
-- each additional 5 years"; s 8A(2)(a)(i) "13 weeks' long service leave in respect of every period of 10 years".
`eight and two-thirds weeks` MEANS 26 DIVIDED BY 3
`four and one-third weeks` MEANS 13 DIVIDED BY 3
`thirteen weeks` MEANS 13

-- s 9(2) and (3) count "a period equalling 1/60th" (or "1/40th") of a period of years; a year is taken as 52 weeks
-- (fork F7), which makes 1/60th of 10 years 8 2/3 weeks and 1/40th of 10 years 13 weeks, as in ss 8 and 8A.
`weeks in a year` MEANS 52

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Does the Act apply to me (ss 2, 3, 7)`

-- s 2(1) "employee means a person who is employed by an employer to do any work for hire or reward, and includes an
-- apprentice or any other person whose contract of employment requires him to learn or be taught any business";
-- "employer ... includes the Crown". s 3 "This Act does not apply to an employee who - (a) is an employee within the
-- meaning of the Long Service Leave (State Employees) Act 1994; or (b) . . . (c) is entitled to long service leave under
-- Division 1 of Part 7 of the Local Government (Building and Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1993." s 7(1): the Secretary
-- may exempt an employer, in respect of a class of employees, where a scheme gives benefits not less favourable; s 7(2):
-- for up to 5 years at a time. The Act has no provision on where the employment must be (finding 1).
DECLARE `How the Act applies to me` IS ONE OF
    `the Act applies to me`
    `the Act applies to me, but the Secretary has exempted my employer for employees like me because a long service leave scheme gives benefits no less favourable (s 7)`
    `the Act does not apply to me: I am a State employee under the Long Service Leave (State Employees) Act 1994 (s 3(a))`
    `the Act does not apply to me: I am entitled to long service leave under the Local Government (Building and Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1993 (s 3(c))`
    `the Act does not apply to me: I am not employed by an employer for hire or reward, nor an apprentice, so I am not an employee (s 2(1))`

GIVEN `I am employed for hire or reward or as an apprentice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am a State employee under the 1994 Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I have local government long service leave` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an exemption covers me` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `How the Act applies to me`
`ss 2, 3, 7 -- how the Act applies` `I am employed for hire or reward or as an apprentice` `I am a State employee under the 1994 Act` `I have local government long service leave` `an exemption covers me` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `I am employed for hire or reward or as an apprentice` THEN `the Act does not apply to me: I am not employed by an employer for hire or reward, nor an apprentice, so I am not an employee (s 2(1))`
           IF `I am a State employee under the 1994 Act` THEN `the Act does not apply to me: I am a State employee under the Long Service Leave (State Employees) Act 1994 (s 3(a))`
           IF `I have local government long service leave` THEN `the Act does not apply to me: I am entitled to long service leave under the Local Government (Building and Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1993 (s 3(c))`
           IF `an exemption covers me` THEN `the Act applies to me, but the Secretary has exempted my employer for employees like me because a long service leave scheme gives benefits no less favourable (s 7)`
           OTHERWISE `the Act applies to me`

@export Does Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 apply to me (ss 2, 3, 7)? It covers anyone employed for hire or reward, including apprentices and Crown employees, except State employees under the Long Service Leave (State Employees) Act 1994 and those with local government long service leave; an employer the Secretary has exempted gives leave under its own scheme instead.
GIVEN `I am employed for hire or reward or as an apprentice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are employed by an employer (a business, a person or the Crown) to do work for pay, or are an apprentice or trainee whose contract requires you to be taught
      `I am a State employee under the 1994 Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an employee within the meaning of the Long Service Leave (State Employees) Act 1994 (most Tasmanian State Service and State authority employees)
      `I have local government long service leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are entitled to long service leave under Division 1 of Part 7 of the Local Government (Building and Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1993
      `an exemption covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Secretary has exempted your employer from the Act, for you or a class of employees you belong to, and the exemption (at most 5 years at a time) is still in force
GIVETH A `How the Act applies to me`
`Does the Act apply to me` MEANS
    `ss 2, 3, 7 -- how the Act applies` `I am employed for hire or reward or as an apprentice` `I am a State employee under the 1994 Act` `I have local government long service leave` `an exemption covers me`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Am I a mining employee (ss 2, 2A, Schedule 2)`

-- s 2(1) "mining employee means an employee who is employed in, at, or about a metalliferous mine"; "metalliferous mine
-- means - (a) a place, open cut, quarry, shaft, tunnel, drive, level or other excavation ... in or by which an operation is
-- carried on for or in connection with the purpose of obtaining a mineral substance ...; or (b) a place adjoining [such] a
-- mine ... on which a product of that mine is stacked, stored, crushed, or otherwise treated - and includes - (c) a place
-- where 2 or more men are employed in connection with prospecting operations for ... a mineral substance ...; and (d) so
-- much of the surface of a place and the buildings, workshops, change-houses, structures, and works ... surrounding or
-- adjacent to the ... mine ... as are occupied, together with the mine, for the purposes of or in connection with the
-- working of the mine ...". s 2A(1) "The several substances specified in Schedule 2 are mineral substances within the
-- meaning of this Act." A substance not in Schedule 2: JUST FALSE (s 2A hands the definition to the Schedule; fork F3).
DECLARE `Kind of place where I work` IS ONE OF
    `a place, open cut, quarry, shaft, tunnel or other excavation where a mineral substance is obtained`
    `a place next to such a mine where its product is stacked, stored, crushed or treated`
    `a place where 2 or more people are employed prospecting or exploring for a mineral substance`
    `the surface, buildings, workshops or works around such a mine, occupied with it for working it`
    `none of these`

-- Schedule 2 - Mineral Substances (s 2A), items 1 to 44.
DECLARE `Substance` IS ONE OF
    Alumina, Antimony, Arsenic, `Arsenical pyrites`, Bauxite, Bismuth, Cadmium, Chromite, Cinnabar, Cobalt, Columbium
    Copper, Galena, Gold, Ilmenite, Iron, `Iron-ore`, Ironstone, Lead, Manganese, Mercury, Molybdenite, Nickel, Osmiridium
    `Oxide of iron`, Pitchblende, `Platinoid minerals`, Platinum, Plumbago, `Radioactive minerals`, Rutile, Scheelite
    Silver, Sulphur, Tantalum, Tin, Titanium, `Tungsten and its ores`, Vanadium, Wolfram, Wulfenite, Zinc, Zircon, Zirconia
    `a substance not listed in Schedule 2 (for example coal, limestone, gravel or sand)`

GIVEN `I am employed in, at or about the place` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the place` IS A `Kind of place where I work`
      `the substance` IS A `Substance`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2 -- I am a mining employee` `I am employed in, at or about the place` `the place` `the substance` MEANS
    `yes or no` (    `I am employed in, at or about the place`
                 AND `the place is of a kind in the definition`
                 AND `the substance is in Schedule 2`)
    WHERE
        `the place is of a kind in the definition` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the place`
            WHEN `none of these` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE
        `the substance is in Schedule 2` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the substance`
            WHEN `a substance not listed in Schedule 2 (for example coal, limestone, gravel or sand)` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

@export Am I a mining employee under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (ss 2, 2A, Schedule 2)? A mining employee works in, at or about a metalliferous mine: a mine, quarry or excavation (or a place next to it, a prospecting site with 2 or more workers, or its surface works) for one of the 44 substances in Schedule 2. Mining employees get 13 weeks for each 10 years instead of 8 2/3 weeks.
GIVEN `I am employed in, at or about the place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are employed in, at or about the place
      `the place` IS A `Kind of place where I work` @desc What kind of place it is: a mine, open cut, quarry, shaft, tunnel or other excavation where a mineral is obtained; a place next to such a mine where its product is stacked, stored, crushed or treated; a place where 2 or more people are employed prospecting or exploring for a mineral; the surface, buildings or works around a mine occupied with it for working it; or none of these
      `the substance` IS A `Substance` @desc The mineral the place is worked for, as listed in Schedule 2 (alumina, antimony, arsenic, bauxite, copper, gold, iron-ore, lead, nickel, silver, tin, tungsten, zinc and others), or a substance not listed (coal, limestone, gravel, sand)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a mining employee` MEANS
    `s 2 -- I am a mining employee` `I am employed in, at or about the place` `the place` `the substance`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Continuous employment (ss 2(2), 5)`

-- The years of continuous employment from the first day, the last day counted, less the days of absence that are not
-- counted (s 5(2)); those days push the first day later by as many days (fork F2). The part of the last year is kept as a
-- fraction, because ss 8(2)(a)(iii), (b), 8A(2) and 9(2), (3) prorate by "the number of years" (fork F2).
GIVEN `the first day of my employment` IS A DATE, `the day asked about` IS A DATE, `days that are not counted` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 5 -- my years of continuous employment` `the first day of my employment` `the day asked about` `days that are not counted` MEANS
    `the years with the part year from` (`the date` (`not below zero` `days that are not counted`) `days after` `the first day of my employment`) `to` `the day asked about`

@export How many years of continuous employment with my employer do I have, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 5)? Counted from your first day (including service with a previous owner of the business, a related company, or an apprenticeship that counts), with the part of the current year as a fraction, less absences that are not counted; the other questions use this number.
GIVEN `the first day of my employment` IS A DATE @desc The first day of your continuous employment with your employer (or with a previous owner of the business, a related company, or as their apprentice, if that service counts)
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (today, your last day of employment, or the day of death), counted as a day of employment
      `days that are not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc The total days of breaks that keep your employment continuous but are not counted (maternity leave, industrial disputes, re-employment gaps, stand-downs, other leave by your employer's leave such as unpaid leave; see the question on breaks); 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How many years of continuous employment do I have` MEANS
    `s 5 -- my years of continuous employment` `the first day of my employment` `the day asked about` `days that are not counted`

-- s 5(1) "employment ... shall be deemed to be continuous notwithstanding - (a) the taking of any annual leave or long
-- service leave; (b) any absence from work ... on a public holiday ...; (c) any absence from work on account of illness or
-- injury that has been certified as necessary by a medical practitioner; (ca) the taking of any maternity leave ...; (d)
-- any interruption or ending of the employment by the employer, if ... made with the intention of avoiding obligations in
-- respect of long service leave or annual leave; (e) any interruption arising ... from an industrial dispute, but only if
-- the employee returns to work in accordance with the terms of settlement of the dispute; (f) any absence ... by leave of
-- the employer, for ... attending a meeting of a committee established under the Training and Workforce Development Act
-- 2013; (g) the termination of the employment ... for any reason other than on account of slackness of trade, but only if
-- he is re-employed by the same employer within 3 months after the date of that termination; (h) the standing down for a
-- period not exceeding 6 months of an employee on account of slackness of trade, or the termination of employment of an
-- employee who returns to work within a period not exceeding 6 months after the termination ... on account of slackness of
-- trade, but only if the return to work ... is made within 14 days after - (i) receiving from the employer an offer of
-- re-employment; or (ii) the date on which the employer posts ... by registered letter ... a notice to resume work; (i)
-- any absence ... for ... complying with a summons to appear as a juror; (ii) appearing to give evidence ...; (iii) ...
-- appear[ing] ... as a party ... or as a witness ...; or (j) any other absence of the employee from work by leave of the
-- employer." s 5(10) "A period of service ... as a member of the naval, military, or air forces (other than as a member of
-- the permanent force) of the Commonwealth shall be deemed to be employment with the employer by whom the employee was last
-- employed ...". An unlisted break: NOTHING; s 5(1)(h) needs both limits (fork F12).
DECLARE `What interrupted my employment` IS ONE OF
    `annual leave or long service leave`
    `a public holiday, in accordance with my terms of employment`
    `illness or injury, certified as necessary by a medical practitioner`
    `maternity leave, in accordance with my terms of employment`
    `my employer interrupted or ended my employment to avoid long service leave or annual leave obligations`
    `an industrial dispute`
    `a meeting of a Training and Workforce Development Act 2013 committee, with my employer's leave`
    `my employment ended for a reason other than slackness of trade, and the same employer re-employed me`
    `I was stood down, or my employment ended, because of slackness of trade, and I returned`
    `jury service, or appearing before a court or other body as a witness or a party`
    `another absence with my employer's leave (for example unpaid leave)`
    `service in the naval, military or air forces of the Commonwealth, other than the permanent forces`
    `something else (for example an absence without my employer's leave)`

GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What interrupted my employment`
      `I returned in accordance with the settlement of the dispute` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day it began` IS A DATE
      `the day I returned` IS A DATE
      `the day I received the offer or the notice was posted` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(1), (10) -- my employment stays continuous` `what happened` `I returned in accordance with the settlement of the dispute` `the day it began` `the day I returned` `the day I received the offer or the notice was posted` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what happened`
    WHEN `an industrial dispute` THEN `yes or no` `I returned in accordance with the settlement of the dispute`
    WHEN `my employment ended for a reason other than slackness of trade, and the same employer re-employed me` THEN
        `yes or no` (`the day` `the day I returned` `is on or before` (`the same day` 3 `months after` `the day it began`))
    WHEN `I was stood down, or my employment ended, because of slackness of trade, and I returned` THEN
        `yes or no` (    (`the day` `the day I returned` `is on or before` (`the same day` 6 `months after` `the day it began`))
                     AND (`the day` `the day I returned` `is on or before` (`the date` 14 `days after` `the day I received the offer or the notice was posted`)))
    WHEN `something else (for example an absence without my employer's leave)` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 5(1) lists what does not break continuity and does not say what does; s 5(3) may still apply)
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@export Did a break keep my employment continuous, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 5(1), (10))? Leave, public holidays, certified illness or injury, maternity leave, an employer's evasion, jury or witness duty, other leave by your employer's leave and part-time military service do not break it; an industrial dispute does not if you returned under the settlement; a termination does not if you were re-employed within 3 months; a stand-down or termination for slackness of trade does not if you returned within 6 months and within 14 days of the offer or the posted notice.
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What interrupted my employment` @desc What interrupted your employment: annual or long service leave; a public holiday; certified illness or injury; maternity leave; your employer ending or interrupting it to avoid leave obligations; an industrial dispute; a training committee meeting; a termination (not for slackness of trade) followed by re-employment by the same employer; a stand-down or termination for slackness of trade followed by your return; jury or witness duty; other leave your employer gave (such as unpaid leave); part-time or reserve military service; or something else
      `I returned in accordance with the settlement of the dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an industrial dispute, whether you returned to work in accordance with the terms of settlement of the dispute (otherwise answer no)
      `the day it began` IS A DATE @desc The day the interruption began (for a termination, the day the employment ended; for a stand-down, the first day of it)
      `the day I returned` IS A DATE @desc The day you were re-employed or returned to work (any day if not relevant)
      `the day I received the offer or the notice was posted` IS A DATE @desc For slackness of trade only: the day you received your employer's offer of re-employment, or the day your employer posted a notice to resume work by registered letter to your last-known address (any day otherwise)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the break keep my employment continuous` MEANS
    `s 5(1), (10) -- my employment stays continuous` `what happened` `I returned in accordance with the settlement of the dispute` `the day it began` `the day I returned` `the day I received the offer or the notice was posted`

-- s 5(2) "In calculating the period of continuous employment ..., an interruption or absence of any of the kinds to which
-- paragraphs (a), (b), (c), (d), (f), and (i) of subsection (1) relate shall be counted as part of the period of his
-- employment, but an interruption or absence of any of the kinds to which paragraphs (ca), (e), (g), (h), and (j) of that
-- subsection relate shall not be counted as part of the period of his employment." s 5(10): reserve service "shall be
-- deemed to be employment".
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What interrupted my employment`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(2), (10) -- the break counts towards my years` `what happened` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what happened`
    WHEN `annual leave or long service leave` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a public holiday, in accordance with my terms of employment` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `illness or injury, certified as necessary by a medical practitioner` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `my employer interrupted or ended my employment to avoid long service leave or annual leave obligations` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a meeting of a Training and Workforce Development Act 2013 committee, with my employer's leave` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `jury service, or appearing before a court or other body as a witness or a party` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `service in the naval, military or air forces of the Commonwealth, other than the permanent forces` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `maternity leave, in accordance with my terms of employment` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `an industrial dispute` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `my employment ended for a reason other than slackness of trade, and the same employer re-employed me` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `I was stood down, or my employment ended, because of slackness of trade, and I returned` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `another absence with my employer's leave (for example unpaid leave)` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 5(2) sorts only the kinds of break s 5(1) lists)

@export Does a break count towards my years of service, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 5(2), (10))? Annual and long service leave, public holidays, certified illness or injury (including time off on workers' compensation with a medical certificate), an employer's evasion, a training committee meeting, jury or witness duty and part-time military service count; maternity leave, industrial disputes, the gap before re-employment, stand-downs and other leave (such as unpaid leave) keep the employment continuous but do not count.
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What interrupted my employment` @desc What the break was (the same choices as the question on whether a break kept your employment continuous)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the break count towards my years` MEANS
    `s 5(2), (10) -- the break counts towards my years` `what happened`

-- s 5(3) "Without limiting subsections (1) and (2), where an employee is regularly employed by an employer for not less
-- than 32 hours in each consecutive period of 4 weeks, the employee shall be deemed ... to be continuously employed by the
-- employer, notwithstanding - (a) that any of the employment is not full-time employment; (b) that the employee was so
-- employed under 2 or more contracts ...; (c) that ... the employee would be regarded as being engaged in casual
-- employment; or (d) that the employee engaged in other employment during that period." "Regularly" is a judgment, asked
-- as a fact. The "where" condition fails: JUST FALSE (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)).
GIVEN `I am regularly employed by this employer` IS A BOOLEAN, `the fewest hours I worked in any 4-week period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(3) -- I am deemed continuously employed although part-time or casual` `I am regularly employed by this employer` `the fewest hours I worked in any 4-week period` MEANS
    `yes or no` (`I am regularly employed by this employer` AND (`the fewest hours I worked in any 4-week period` AT LEAST 32))

@export As a casual or part-time worker, am I continuously employed under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 5(3))? If you are regularly employed by the employer for at least 32 hours in each consecutive 4-week period, you are deemed continuously employed even if the work is casual or part-time, under several contracts, or alongside other jobs.
GIVEN `I am regularly employed by this employer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you work for this employer on a regular basis (a judgment; answer as the facts suggest)
      `the fewest hours I worked in any 4-week period` IS A NUMBER @desc The fewest hours you worked for this employer in any of the consecutive 4-week periods over the time asked about
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`As a casual or part-time worker am I continuously employed` MEANS
    `s 5(3) -- I am deemed continuously employed although part-time or casual` `I am regularly employed by this employer` `the fewest hours I worked in any 4-week period`

-- s 2(2): where an employee's employment in or about a place ends and "not later than the expiration of a period of 2
-- months from the date on which that employment was so terminated, the employee becomes employed in or about that place
-- in the business of some other employer", the business "shall ... be deemed to have been transmitted" to the new
-- employer "if the business ... is of the same, or substantially the same, kind". s 5(4): on a transmission (any
-- "transfer, conveyance, assignment, or succession", s 2(1)), the period with the transmittee includes the period with the
-- transmittor. s 5(5), (6): a transfer to a related corporation (s 50 of the Corporations Act). s 5(7): directors or
-- management substantially the same and the employee "believes on reasonable grounds that he has remained in employment
-- with the same employer". s 5(9): "Where the employment of an employee who is apprenticed to an employer has been
-- continued by that employer upon or at any time within 3 months after the completion of the apprenticeship, the period of
-- the apprenticeship shall be counted". None of these: the earlier service is not with this employer (fork F4).
DECLARE `How my earlier service is linked to this employment` IS ONE OF
    `the business I worked in was transferred to my present employer (sold, assigned or passed on) and I became its employee`
    `my job ended and another employer took me on at the same place, in the same or substantially the same kind of business`
    `I transferred from one corporation to a related corporation`
    `I transferred between corporations with substantially the same directors or management, and I believed on reasonable grounds I had stayed with the same employer`
    `I finished my apprenticeship with this employer and it kept me on`
    `none of these`

GIVEN `the link` IS A `How my earlier service is linked to this employment`
      `the day the earlier employment ended` IS A DATE
      `the day this employment began` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`ss 2(2), 5(4), (5), (7), (9) -- my earlier service counts` `the link` `the day the earlier employment ended` `the day this employment began` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the link`
    WHEN `my job ended and another employer took me on at the same place, in the same or substantially the same kind of business` THEN
        `yes or no` (`the day` `the day this employment began` `is on or before` (`the same day` 2 `months after` `the day the earlier employment ended`))
    WHEN `I finished my apprenticeship with this employer and it kept me on` THEN
        `yes or no` (`the day` `the day this employment began` `is on or before` (`the same day` 3 `months after` `the day the earlier employment ended`))
    WHEN `none of these` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@export Does my earlier service count towards my long service leave with my present employer, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (ss 2(2), 5(4) to (9))? It counts if the business was transferred to your employer, if another employer took you on at the same place in the same kind of business within 2 months, if you moved between related companies (or companies under the same management, believing you stayed with the same employer), or if your employer kept you on within 3 months after your apprenticeship.
GIVEN `the link` IS A `How my earlier service is linked to this employment` @desc How the earlier service is linked: the business was transferred to your present employer; another employer took you on at the same place in the same kind of business; a transfer between related companies; a transfer between companies with substantially the same directors or management, believing you stayed with the same employer; your employer kept you on after your apprenticeship; or none of these
      `the day the earlier employment ended` IS A DATE @desc The day your earlier employment ended (for an apprenticeship, the day you completed it)
      `the day this employment began` IS A DATE @desc The day your present employment began (or continued after the apprenticeship)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my earlier service count` MEANS
    `ss 2(2), 5(4), (5), (7), (9) -- my earlier service counts` `the link` `the day the earlier employment ended` `the day this employment began`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `The age for retirement (ss 2, 8(3A), 8A(3A))`

-- s 2(1) "age for retirement means - (a) in a case where an age for retirement is prescribed in an industrial award that
-- is applicable to an employee, or is fixed by the terms of an employee's contract of employment - the age so prescribed
-- or fixed; or (b) in any other case - the age of 65 years, in the case of a male, or 60 years in the case of a female".
-- s 8(3A), 8A(3A): an employee who "terminates his employment on or after becoming eligible for a service pension under
-- the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986 of the Commonwealth shall be deemed to have attained the age for retirement".
-- Paragraph (b) names only male and female; for anyone else the text is silent, unless the age passes both (finding 3).
DECLARE `The sex the default age turns on` IS ONE OF `male`, `female`, `neither, or not stated`

GIVEN `an award or my contract fixes an age for retirement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the age fixed` IS A NUMBER
      `my sex` IS A `The sex the default age turns on`
      `my age` IS A NUMBER
      `I left on or after becoming eligible for a veterans' service pension` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`ss 2, 8(3A) -- I have attained the age for retirement` `an award or my contract fixes an age for retirement` `the age fixed` `my sex` `my age` `I left on or after becoming eligible for a veterans' service pension` MEANS
    IF `I left on or after becoming eligible for a veterans' service pension`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF `an award or my contract fixes an age for retirement`
         THEN `yes or no` (`my age` AT LEAST `the age fixed`)
         ELSE CONSIDER `my sex`
              WHEN `male` THEN `yes or no` (`my age` AT LEAST 65)
              WHEN `female` THEN `yes or no` (`my age` AT LEAST 60)
              WHEN `neither, or not stated` THEN
                  BRANCH IF `my age` AT LEAST 65 THEN JUST TRUE
                         IF `my age` LESS THAN 60 THEN JUST FALSE
                         OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 2(1) "age for retirement" (b) gives an age only for a male or a female)

@export Have I reached the "age for retirement" under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (ss 2, 8(3A))? It is the age an applicable award or your contract fixes; otherwise the Act says 65 for a male and 60 for a female. Leaving on or after becoming eligible for a veterans' service pension counts as reaching it. It matters for pro rata leave between 7 and 10 years (5 and 10 for mining).
GIVEN `an award or my contract fixes an age for retirement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an industrial award that applies to you, or your contract of employment, fixes an age for retirement
      `the age fixed` IS A NUMBER @desc The age for retirement the award or contract fixes (any number if none)
      `my sex` IS A `The sex the default age turns on` @desc Your sex, which the Act's default ages turn on: male (65), female (60), or neither or not stated
      `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years on the day asked about (for example the day your employment ended)
      `I left on or after becoming eligible for a veterans' service pension` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ended your employment on or after becoming eligible for a service pension under the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986 of the Commonwealth
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Have I reached the age for retirement` MEANS
    `ss 2, 8(3A) -- I have attained the age for retirement` `an award or my contract fixes an age for retirement` `the age fixed` `my sex` `my age` `I left on or after becoming eligible for a veterans' service pension`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `How much long service leave (ss 7A, 8, 8A)`

-- s 8(3), 8A(3) "Subsection (2) (b) applies to - (a) an employee who attains the age for retirement; (b) an employee whose
-- employment is terminated on account of illness of such a nature as to justify the termination of that employment; (c) an
-- employee who terminates his employment on account of incapacity or domestic or other pressing necessity of such a nature
-- as to justify the termination of that employment; and (d) an employee whose employment is terminated by his employer for
-- any reason other than the serious and wilful misconduct of the employee." Whether the illness or necessity "justifies"
-- the termination, and whether misconduct was "serious and wilful", are judgments asked as facts.
DECLARE `How my employment stands` IS ONE OF
    `I am still employed`
    `my employer ended it because of an illness that justified ending it`
    `I ended it because of incapacity, or a domestic or other pressing necessity, that justified ending it`
    `my employer ended it for a reason other than my serious and wilful misconduct`
    `my employer ended it for my serious and wilful misconduct`
    `I resigned or retired for another reason`

GIVEN `how it stands` IS A `How my employment stands`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `my employment has ended` `how it stands` IF
    CONSIDER `how it stands`
    WHEN `I am still employed` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 8(3)(b) to (d), 8A(3)(b) to (d).
GIVEN `how it stands` IS A `How my employment stands`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `my employment ended in a way that s 8(3) names` `how it stands` IF
    CONSIDER `how it stands`
    WHEN `my employer ended it because of an illness that justified ending it` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I ended it because of incapacity, or a domestic or other pressing necessity, that justified ending it` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `my employer ended it for a reason other than my serious and wilful misconduct` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 7A "Subject to this Act, an employee is entitled to long service leave on ordinary pay in respect of continuous
-- employment with an employer."
-- s 8(2) (an employee who is not a mining employee) "(a) on the completion by an employee of at least 10 years' continuous
-- employment with his employer - (i) 8 2/3 weeks' long service leave in respect of the first 10 years' continuous
-- employment ...; and (ii) 4 1/3 weeks' long service leave in respect of each additional 5 years' continuous employment
-- ...; and (iii) on the termination of his employment, an additional period ... in respect of the number of years'
-- continuous employment ... since the last accrual of entitlement ..., such period ... as bears the same proportion to
-- 8 2/3 weeks as that number of years bears to 10 years; or (b) in the case of an employee to whom this paragraph applies
-- by virtue of subsection (3) who has completed 7 years', but has not completed 10 years', continuous employment ... such
-- period of long service leave as bears the same proportion to 8 2/3 weeks as the total period of the employee's
-- continuous employment with his employer bears to 10 years."
-- s 8A(2) (a mining employee) "(a) on the completion ... of at least 10 years' continuous employment ... - (i) 13 weeks'
-- long service leave in respect of every period of 10 years' continuous employment ...; and (ii) on the termination of his
-- employment, an additional period ... [that] bears the same proportion to 13 weeks as that number of years bears to 10
-- years; or (b) in the case of an employee to whom this paragraph applies by virtue of subsection (3) who has completed 5
-- years', but has not completed 10 years', continuous employment ..., such a period ... as bears the same proportion to 13
-- weeks as the total period of the employee's continuous employment ... bears to 10 years."
-- Paragraph (b) applies to an employee who "attains the age for retirement" whether or not the employment has ended (fork
-- F5). The total weeks earned, before leave taken is deducted.
GIVEN `I am a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `years` IS A NUMBER
      `how it stands` IS A `How my employment stands`
      `I have attained the age for retirement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`ss 8(2), 8A(2) -- the weeks of long service leave I have become entitled to` `I am a mining employee` `years` `how it stands` `I have attained the age for retirement` MEANS
    IF `years` AT LEAST 10
    THEN IF `my employment has ended` `how it stands`
         THEN `the weeks accrued` PLUS `the part since the last accrual`
         ELSE `the weeks accrued`
    ELSE IF     (`years` AT LEAST `the years paragraph (b) needs`)
            AND (`I have attained the age for retirement` OR `my employment ended in a way that s 8(3) names` `how it stands`)
         THEN (`years` DIVIDED BY 10) TIMES `the weeks for 10 years`
         ELSE 0
    WHERE
        `the weeks for 10 years` MEANS
            IF `I am a mining employee` THEN `thirteen weeks` ELSE `eight and two-thirds weeks`
        `the years paragraph (b) needs` MEANS
            IF `I am a mining employee` THEN 5 ELSE 7
        `the additional 5-year periods` MEANS FLOOR ((`years` MINUS 10) DIVIDED BY 5)
        `the 10-year periods` MEANS FLOOR (`years` DIVIDED BY 10)
        `the weeks accrued` MEANS
            IF `I am a mining employee`
            THEN `thirteen weeks` TIMES `the 10-year periods`
            ELSE `eight and two-thirds weeks` PLUS (`four and one-third weeks` TIMES `the additional 5-year periods`)
        `the years at the last accrual` MEANS
            IF `I am a mining employee`
            THEN 10 TIMES `the 10-year periods`
            ELSE 10 PLUS (5 TIMES `the additional 5-year periods`)
        `the part since the last accrual` MEANS
            ((`years` MINUS `the years at the last accrual`) DIVIDED BY 10) TIMES `the weeks for 10 years`

-- The weeks still owed: the weeks earned less the weeks already taken or paid out (ss 6(2)(b), 10, 12(4)).
GIVEN `I am a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `years` IS A NUMBER
      `how it stands` IS A `How my employment stands`
      `I have attained the age for retirement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `weeks already taken or paid out` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`ss 8, 8A -- the weeks of long service leave owed to me` `I am a mining employee` `years` `how it stands` `I have attained the age for retirement` `weeks already taken or paid out` MEANS
    `not below zero` ((`ss 8(2), 8A(2) -- the weeks of long service leave I have become entitled to` `I am a mining employee` `years` `how it stands` `I have attained the age for retirement`) MINUS `weeks already taken or paid out`)

@export How many weeks of long service leave am I entitled to, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (ss 8, 8A)? After 10 years: 8 2/3 weeks, then 4 1/3 weeks for each further 5 years (mining employees: 13 weeks for each 10 years); when the employment ends, a pro rata part for the years since the last block. Between 7 and 10 years (5 and 10 for mining), a pro rata amount if you have reached the age for retirement, or your employment ends because of a justifying illness, incapacity or pressing necessity, or your employer ends it other than for serious and wilful misconduct. Less what you have already taken or been paid out.
GIVEN `I am a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a mining employee (see the question on mining employees)
      `years` IS A NUMBER @desc Your years of continuous employment with your employer, with any part year as a fraction (for example 12.5); see the question on years of continuous employment
      `how it stands` IS A `How my employment stands` @desc Whether you are still employed, or how the employment ended: your employer ended it because of an illness that justified it; you ended it because of incapacity or a domestic or other pressing necessity that justified it; your employer ended it other than for serious and wilful misconduct; your employer ended it for serious and wilful misconduct; or you resigned or retired for another reason
      `I have attained the age for retirement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reached the age for retirement (see that question)
      `weeks already taken or paid out` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks of long service leave you have already taken, or been paid instead of taking, for this employment; 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How many weeks of long service leave am I entitled to` MEANS
    `ss 8, 8A -- the weeks of long service leave owed to me` `I am a mining employee` `years` `how it stands` `I have attained the age for retirement` `weeks already taken or paid out`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Taking the leave (s 12(1), (2), (3), (5), (10))`

-- s 12(10) "Except in the case of an employee to whom subsection (4) relates, nothing in this Act requires an employer to
-- grant to an employee leave to which the employee has become entitled under this Act, unless - (a) in the case of an
-- employee who is not a mining employee, the period of leave to which he has become entitled is equal to or greater than
-- 8 2/3 weeks in respect of his first period of entitlement and 4 1/3 weeks in respect of a subsequent period of
-- entitlement; and (b) in the case of a mining employee, ... equal to or greater than 13 weeks in respect of each of his
-- periods of entitlement." s 12(1): when that is met, the leave "shall be granted by the employer as soon as practicable
-- after the employee becomes entitled to it, having regard to the needs of the employer's establishment" (a judgment, not
-- decided), but it may be postponed by agreement or by the Secretary's direction, and (1)(b) "in no case is any
-- entitlement to long service leave lost". "Unless" not met: JUST FALSE (flat "nothing ... requires").
GIVEN `I am a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I have had an earlier period of entitlement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `weeks I am entitled to and have not taken` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(1), (10) -- my employer must grant the leave` `I am a mining employee` `I have had an earlier period of entitlement` `weeks I am entitled to and have not taken` MEANS
    `yes or no` (`weeks I am entitled to and have not taken` AT LEAST `the least period`)
    WHERE
        `the least period` MEANS
            IF `I am a mining employee`
            THEN `thirteen weeks`
            ELSE IF `I have had an earlier period of entitlement`
                 THEN `four and one-third weeks`
                 ELSE `eight and two-thirds weeks`

@export While I am still employed, must my employer grant me my long service leave, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 12(1), (10))? Once the leave you are entitled to is at least 8 2/3 weeks (the first time) or 4 1/3 weeks (later times), or 13 weeks for a mining employee, your employer must grant it as soon as practicable having regard to its needs, unless you agree, or the Secretary directs, to postpone it; the entitlement is never lost.
GIVEN `I am a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a mining employee
      `I have had an earlier period of entitlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have become entitled to long service leave with this employer before (after your first 10 years)
      `weeks I am entitled to and have not taken` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks of long service leave you are entitled to and have not taken
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my employer grant me my leave` MEANS
    `s 12(1), (10) -- my employer must grant the leave` `I am a mining employee` `I have had an earlier period of entitlement` `weeks I am entitled to and have not taken`

-- s 12(1)(a) "the taking of the leave may be postponed to such date as is agreed upon between the employer and the
-- employee or, in default of agreement, as the Secretary, having regard to the problems involved, directs, but no
-- direction by the Secretary under this paragraph shall require the long service leave to commence before the expiration
-- of 6 months after the date of the direction". The 6 months expire at the end of the same day 6 months on; the leave can
-- be required to start no earlier than the next day (forks F1, F6).
GIVEN `the day of the direction` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 12(1)(a) -- the earliest day a direction can start my leave` `the day of the direction` MEANS
    `the date` 1 `days after` (`the same day` 6 `months after` `the day of the direction`)

@export If my employer and I cannot agree when I take my long service leave and the Secretary gives a direction, what is the earliest day the direction can make my leave start, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 12(1)(a))? A direction cannot require the leave to start before 6 months after the date of the direction have expired.
GIVEN `the day of the direction` IS A DATE @desc The date of the Secretary's direction
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the earliest day a direction can start my leave` MEANS
    `s 12(1)(a) -- the earliest day a direction can start my leave` `the day of the direction`

-- s 12(2) "Nothing in subsection (1), or in any agreement or direction made under this section, or in a determination made
-- on an appeal against such a direction, requires an employee to take any long service leave ... during any period in
-- respect of which he is paid, or is entitled to be paid, any sum by way of workers' compensation and in which he is
-- unable to work in his ordinary employment by reason of the injury or disablement in respect of which he is entitled to
-- that workers' compensation." s 12(3): the agreement, direction or determination is then "of no effect with respect to
-- that period", but a further one may be made. s 2(1) "workers' compensation means compensation payable under - (a) the
-- Workers Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1988; or (ab) the Asbestos-Related Diseases (Occupational Exposure)
-- Compensation Act 2011; or (b) the Workers' (Occupational Diseases) Relief Fund Act 1954 or a scheme substituted for the
-- provisions thereof under section 41 of that Act." Compensation under another law is outside the "means" definition:
-- JUST FALSE (finding 6, fork F13).
DECLARE `The law I am paid compensation under` IS ONE OF
    `the Workers Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1988 (Tas)`
    `the Asbestos-Related Diseases (Occupational Exposure) Compensation Act 2011 (Tas)`
    `the Workers' (Occupational Diseases) Relief Fund Act 1954 (Tas), or a scheme substituted under it`
    `another law (for example a Commonwealth scheme such as Comcare)`
    `I am not paid, or entitled to be paid, compensation`

GIVEN `the law` IS A `The law I am paid compensation under`
      `I am unable to work in my ordinary employment because of that injury or disablement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(2), (3) -- I need not take my leave during this period` `the law` `I am unable to work in my ordinary employment because of that injury or disablement` MEANS
    `yes or no` (`the compensation is workers' compensation` AND `I am unable to work in my ordinary employment because of that injury or disablement`)
    WHERE
        `the compensation is workers' compensation` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the law`
            WHEN `another law (for example a Commonwealth scheme such as Comcare)` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `I am not paid, or entitled to be paid, compensation` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

@export I am on workers' compensation: can I refuse to take my long service leave during this period, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 12(2), (3))? Nothing in an agreement, a Secretary's direction or an appeal requires you to take long service leave during a period for which you are paid, or entitled to be paid, workers' compensation under the Tasmanian Acts and in which the injury or disablement stops you doing your ordinary work; the agreed or directed dates then have no effect for that period, and new dates may be set.
GIVEN `the law` IS A `The law I am paid compensation under` @desc The law under which you are paid, or entitled to be paid, compensation for the period: the Workers Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1988 (Tas); the Asbestos-Related Diseases (Occupational Exposure) Compensation Act 2011 (Tas); the Workers' (Occupational Diseases) Relief Fund Act 1954 (Tas) or a scheme substituted under it; another law; or no compensation
      `I am unable to work in my ordinary employment because of that injury or disablement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, during the period, the injury or disablement you are compensated for stops you working in your ordinary employment
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`On workers compensation can I refuse to take my leave in this period` MEANS
    `s 12(2), (3) -- I need not take my leave during this period` `the law` `I am unable to work in my ordinary employment because of that injury or disablement`

-- s 12(5) "An employer and employee may agree that any accrued entitlement to long service leave shall be taken in 2
-- periods, but, except in pursuance of an agreement under this subsection, long service leave shall be taken in one
-- period." More than 2 periods, or 2 without agreement: JUST FALSE (flat "shall be taken in one period", "except").
GIVEN `number of periods` IS A NUMBER, `my employer and I agree to 2 periods` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(5) -- I may take my leave in these periods` `number of periods` `my employer and I agree to 2 periods` MEANS
    `yes or no` (    (`number of periods` EQUALS 1)
                  OR (`my employer and I agree to 2 periods` AND (`number of periods` EQUALS 2)))

@export Can I split my long service leave into separate periods, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 12(5))? The leave is taken in one period, or in 2 periods if you and your employer agree; not in more.
GIVEN `number of periods` IS A NUMBER @desc Into how many periods the leave would be split (1 for one continuous period)
      `my employer and I agree to 2 periods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and your employer agree to take the accrued leave in 2 periods
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I split my leave into these periods` MEANS
    `s 12(5) -- I may take my leave in these periods` `number of periods` `my employer and I agree to 2 periods`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Payment instead of leave, by agreement (s 10)`

-- s 10 "Where an employee becomes entitled to a period of long service leave under this Act, he may, by agreement with his
-- employer, elect to accept payment in lieu of the period of long service leave to which he is so entitled and if he so
-- elects the employer may pay him accordingly a sum equal to the amount of his ordinary pay ... of the length of that
-- period of long service leave." Not entitled: JUST FALSE (a "where" condition). Entitled but no agreement: NOTHING (a
-- grant limited by its own wording). s 17 forbids contracting out "except as otherwise expressly provided"; s 10 is such a
-- provision.
GIVEN `I am entitled to a period of long service leave` IS A BOOLEAN, `my employer agrees` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 10 -- I may take payment instead of my leave` `I am entitled to a period of long service leave` `my employer agrees` MEANS
    IF NOT `I am entitled to a period of long service leave`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `my employer agrees`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 10 permits payment in lieu only "by agreement with his employer")

@export Can I be paid out my long service leave instead of taking it, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 10)? Once you are entitled to a period of leave, you may, by agreement with your employer, elect to be paid your ordinary pay for that period instead; the Act gives no right to a payout without that agreement while you are employed.
GIVEN `I am entitled to a period of long service leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have become entitled to a period of long service leave (see the question on how many weeks)
      `my employer agrees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer agrees to pay you instead of you taking the leave
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be paid instead of taking my leave` MEANS
    `s 10 -- I may take payment instead of my leave` `I am entitled to a period of long service leave` `my employer agrees`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Ordinary pay (s 11)`

-- s 11(1): ordinary pay is "the remuneration that he would reasonably be expected to have received in respect of that
-- period from that employment if he had continued throughout that period to have worked therein". (2)(a) the normal weekly
-- hours, no overtime; (c) disability allowances disregarded; (d) board and lodging provided by the employer counted at
-- their cash value, (e) except where provided because the work requires sleeping away from home or other special
-- circumstances; (f) meals, (g) travel and vehicle payments and (h) discretionary bonuses disregarded. (3) pay that depends
-- on results: "the average rate of the remuneration received by him in that employment during the period of 3 months
-- ending on the commencement of that period"; (4) no ordinary rate fixed: the average rate "during the period of 12 months
-- ending on the commencement of the relevant period"; (6) no normal weekly hours fixed: "the average weekly number of
-- hours worked by him in that employment during the period of 12 months". (8) the cash value of board or lodging is that
-- fixed by the terms of employment, or agreed, or determined by the Secretary. The average rate is taken per hour (pay in
-- the period over the hours in it) and the 12 months as 52 weeks (fork F8). A zero divisor: NOTHING.
DECLARE `How my rate of pay is fixed` IS ONE OF
    `my terms fix an ordinary rate for my work`
    `my pay depends on the results I achieve`
    `no ordinary rate is fixed for my work`

GIVEN `how my rate is fixed` IS A `How my rate of pay is fixed`
      `my ordinary hourly rate` IS A NUMBER
      `pay received in the averaging period` IS A NUMBER
      `hours worked in the averaging period` IS A NUMBER
      `my terms fix my normal weekly hours` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my normal weekly hours` IS A NUMBER
      `hours worked in the last 12 months` IS A NUMBER
      `the weekly cash value of board and lodging counted as pay` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 11 -- my weekly ordinary pay` `how my rate is fixed` `my ordinary hourly rate` `pay received in the averaging period` `hours worked in the averaging period` `my terms fix my normal weekly hours` `my normal weekly hours` `hours worked in the last 12 months` `the weekly cash value of board and lodging counted as pay` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how my rate is fixed`
    WHEN `my terms fix an ordinary rate for my work` THEN
        JUST ((`my ordinary hourly rate` TIMES `the weekly hours`) PLUS `the weekly cash value of board and lodging counted as pay`)
    OTHERWISE
        IF `hours worked in the averaging period` GREATER THAN 0
        THEN JUST (((`pay received in the averaging period` DIVIDED BY `hours worked in the averaging period`) TIMES `the weekly hours`) PLUS `the weekly cash value of board and lodging counted as pay`)
        ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: with no hours worked in the averaging period the average rate divides by zero)
    WHERE
        `the weekly hours` MEANS
            IF `my terms fix my normal weekly hours`
            THEN `my normal weekly hours`
            ELSE `hours worked in the last 12 months` DIVIDED BY `weeks in a year`

@export What is my weekly "ordinary pay" for long service leave, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 11)? Your normal weekly hours (or, if none are fixed, your average weekly hours over the last 12 months) at your ordinary rate (or, if your pay depends on results, your average rate over the last 3 months; if no rate is fixed, over the last 12 months), plus the cash value of board and lodging your employer provides as pay. Overtime, disability allowances, meals, travel and vehicle payments and discretionary bonuses are left out.
GIVEN `how my rate is fixed` IS A `How my rate of pay is fixed` @desc How your rate of pay is fixed: your terms fix an ordinary rate; your pay depends on the results you achieve (piecework, commission); or no ordinary rate is fixed
      `my ordinary hourly rate` IS A NUMBER @desc Your ordinary hourly rate in dollars, if your terms fix one (any number otherwise)
      `pay received in the averaging period` IS A NUMBER @desc If your pay depends on results: what you were paid in the 3 months before the leave (or payment) begins; if no rate is fixed: in the 12 months before; leaving out overtime, disability allowances, meals, travel and discretionary bonuses (any number if a rate is fixed)
      `hours worked in the averaging period` IS A NUMBER @desc The ordinary hours you worked in that same 3 or 12 months (any number if a rate is fixed)
      `my terms fix my normal weekly hours` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your terms of employment fix a normal number of hours a week
      `my normal weekly hours` IS A NUMBER @desc Your normal weekly hours, if fixed (any number otherwise)
      `hours worked in the last 12 months` IS A NUMBER @desc If no weekly hours are fixed: the hours you worked in the 12 months before the leave (or payment) begins, leaving out overtime (any number otherwise)
      `the weekly cash value of board and lodging counted as pay` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly cash value of board and lodging your employer provides as part of your pay (as your terms fix it, or as agreed or determined by the Secretary); 0 if none, or if it is provided only because the work is away from home
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is my weekly ordinary pay` MEANS
    `s 11 -- my weekly ordinary pay` `how my rate is fixed` `my ordinary hourly rate` `pay received in the averaging period` `hours worked in the averaging period` `my terms fix my normal weekly hours` `my normal weekly hours` `hours worked in the last 12 months` `the weekly cash value of board and lodging counted as pay`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `When my employment ends (ss 8(2)(a)(iii), (b), 8A(2), 12(4)) -- arises only when the employment ends`

-- s 12(4) "where the employment of an employee is for any reason terminated before he takes any long service leave to
-- which he is entitled, or where any long service leave entitlement accrues to an employee because of the termination of
-- his employment, the employee shall be deemed to have commenced to take his leave on the date of the termination of
-- employment and to be entitled to be paid by his employer ordinary pay in respect of that leave accordingly." The
-- weekly ordinary pay is at the rate on that day (s 11(9) disregards later variations).
GIVEN `I am a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `years` IS A NUMBER
      `how it ended` IS A `How my employment stands`
      `I had attained the age for retirement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `weeks already taken or paid out` IS A NUMBER
      `my weekly ordinary pay` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 12(4) -- what I must be paid when my employment ends` `I am a mining employee` `years` `how it ended` `I had attained the age for retirement` `weeks already taken or paid out` `my weekly ordinary pay` MEANS
    IF `my employment has ended` `how it ended`
    THEN (`ss 8, 8A -- the weeks of long service leave owed to me` `I am a mining employee` `years` `how it ended` `I had attained the age for retirement` `weeks already taken or paid out`) TIMES `my weekly ordinary pay`
    ELSE 0

@export What must my employer pay me for long service leave when my employment ends, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (ss 8, 8A, 12(4))? Your ordinary pay for every week of leave you are entitled to and have not taken, including the pro rata part for the years since your last block (and, between 7 and 10 years, 5 and 10 for mining, if you qualify for pro rata leave); you are treated as starting the leave on the day the employment ends. 0 while you are still employed.
GIVEN `I am a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a mining employee
      `years` IS A NUMBER @desc Your years of continuous employment up to your last day, with any part year as a fraction (for example 12.5)
      `how it ended` IS A `How my employment stands` @desc How the employment ended (the same choices as the question on how many weeks)
      `I had attained the age for retirement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had reached the age for retirement (see that question)
      `weeks already taken or paid out` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks of long service leave you had already taken, or been paid instead of taking; 0 if none
      `my weekly ordinary pay` IS A NUMBER @desc Your weekly ordinary pay in dollars on your last day (see the question on ordinary pay)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What must I be paid when my employment ends` MEANS
    `s 12(4) -- what I must be paid when my employment ends` `I am a mining employee` `years` `how it ended` `I had attained the age for retirement` `weeks already taken or paid out` `my weekly ordinary pay`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `If the employee dies (s 9) -- asked by the personal representative; arises only on a death`

-- s 9(1) "If an employee who is entitled to long service leave under this Act dies before or while taking that leave his
-- employer, unless he has paid to the employee, in advance, a sum equal to the amount of his ordinary pay in respect of
-- the period of that leave, shall pay to the employee's personal representatives a sum equal to the amount of ordinary pay
-- that would have been payable to the employee in respect of the period of long service leave not taken by the employee,
-- less any amount already paid to the employee in respect of any long service leave not taken by him."
-- s 9(2) "Where an employee who is not a mining employee dies while he is still in the continuous employment of an
-- employer after having completed - (a) more than 10 years' continuous employment ..., the employer shall, in addition to
-- any sum payable under subsection (1), pay ... in respect of the period of that continuous employment that is after the
-- last accrual of entitlement to long service leave under section 8 (2) (a), a sum equal to the amount of his ordinary pay
-- for a period equalling 1/60th of the first-mentioned period; or (b) at least 7 years', but less than 10 years', ... a sum
-- equal to the amount of the employee's ordinary pay for a period equalling 1/60th of the period of his continuous
-- employment." s 9(3): for a mining employee, 1/40th, and "at least 5 years', but less than 10 years'".
-- s 9(1) is read as the blocks accrued under s 8(2)(a)(i), (ii) or s 8A(2)(a)(i) (fork F9); a death outside the
-- employment, or before 7 years (5 for mining), gives no s 9(2), (3) sum ("where": JUST FALSE, shown as 0 weeks).
GIVEN `the employee was a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `years at death` IS A NUMBER
      `the employee died while still in the employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `weeks already taken or paid out` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 9 -- the weeks of ordinary pay owed to the estate` `the employee was a mining employee` `years at death` `the employee died while still in the employment` `weeks already taken or paid out` MEANS
    `the weeks under s 9(1)` PLUS `the weeks under s 9(2) or (3)`
    WHERE
        `the 10-year periods` MEANS FLOOR (`years at death` DIVIDED BY 10)
        `the additional 5-year periods` MEANS FLOOR ((`years at death` MINUS 10) DIVIDED BY 5)
        `the weeks accrued` MEANS
            IF `years at death` LESS THAN 10
            THEN 0
            ELSE IF `the employee was a mining employee`
                 THEN `thirteen weeks` TIMES `the 10-year periods`
                 ELSE `eight and two-thirds weeks` PLUS (`four and one-third weeks` TIMES `the additional 5-year periods`)
        `the years at the last accrual` MEANS
            IF `the employee was a mining employee`
            THEN 10 TIMES `the 10-year periods`
            ELSE 10 PLUS (5 TIMES `the additional 5-year periods`)
        `the fraction` MEANS
            IF `the employee was a mining employee` THEN 1 DIVIDED BY 40 ELSE 1 DIVIDED BY 60
        `the years paragraph (b) needs` MEANS
            IF `the employee was a mining employee` THEN 5 ELSE 7
        `the weeks under s 9(1)` MEANS `not below zero` (`the weeks accrued` MINUS `weeks already taken or paid out`)
        `the weeks under s 9(2) or (3)` MEANS
            IF NOT `the employee died while still in the employment`
            THEN 0
            ELSE IF `years at death` GREATER THAN 10
                 THEN ((`years at death` MINUS `the years at the last accrual`) TIMES `weeks in a year`) TIMES `the fraction`
                 ELSE IF `years at death` AT LEAST `the years paragraph (b) needs`
                      THEN IF `years at death` LESS THAN 10
                           THEN (`years at death` TIMES `weeks in a year`) TIMES `the fraction`
                           ELSE 0
                      ELSE 0

@export How many weeks of ordinary pay must the employer pay to a deceased employee's estate, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 9)? Asked by the personal representative. The leave the employee was entitled to and had not taken; plus, if the employee died still in the employment, 1/60th (mining: 1/40th) of the years since the last block of leave (after more than 10 years) or of the whole period (between 7 and 10 years; mining 5 and 10), a year counted as 52 weeks.
GIVEN `the employee was a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employee was a mining employee
      `years at death` IS A NUMBER @desc The employee's years of continuous employment with the employer at death, with any part year as a fraction
      `the employee died while still in the employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employee died while still in the continuous employment of that employer
      `weeks already taken or paid out` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks of long service leave the employee had already taken or been paid out for this employment; 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How many weeks of pay are owed to the estate` MEANS
    `s 9 -- the weeks of ordinary pay owed to the estate` `the employee was a mining employee` `years at death` `the employee died while still in the employment` `weeks already taken or paid out`

GIVEN `the employee was a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `years at death` IS A NUMBER
      `the employee died while still in the employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `weeks already taken or paid out` IS A NUMBER
      `the weekly ordinary pay` IS A NUMBER
      `amount already paid in advance for leave not taken` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 9 -- the sum owed to the estate` `the employee was a mining employee` `years at death` `the employee died while still in the employment` `weeks already taken or paid out` `the weekly ordinary pay` `amount already paid in advance for leave not taken` MEANS
    `not below zero` (((`s 9 -- the weeks of ordinary pay owed to the estate` `the employee was a mining employee` `years at death` `the employee died while still in the employment` `weeks already taken or paid out`) TIMES `the weekly ordinary pay`) MINUS `amount already paid in advance for leave not taken`)

@export How much must the employer pay a deceased employee's personal representative for long service leave, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 9)? The weeks owed to the estate at the employee's weekly ordinary pay, less anything already paid in advance for leave not taken.
GIVEN `the employee was a mining employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employee was a mining employee
      `years at death` IS A NUMBER @desc The employee's years of continuous employment with the employer at death, with any part year as a fraction
      `the employee died while still in the employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employee died while still in the continuous employment of that employer
      `weeks already taken or paid out` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks of long service leave the employee had already taken or been paid out; 0 if none
      `the weekly ordinary pay` IS A NUMBER @desc The employee's weekly ordinary pay in dollars (see the question on ordinary pay; later pay rises are disregarded, s 11(9))
      `amount already paid in advance for leave not taken` IS A NUMBER @desc Any amount the employer had already paid the employee, in advance, for long service leave not taken, in dollars; 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much must be paid to the estate` MEANS
    `s 9 -- the sum owed to the estate` `the employee was a mining employee` `years at death` `the employee died while still in the employment` `weeks already taken or paid out` `the weekly ordinary pay` `amount already paid in advance for leave not taken`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `When I must be paid (s 12(4), (6))`

-- The parties and the act of the rule with a deadline.
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF `my employer`, `me`
DECLARE `What is done` IS ONE OF
    `pay my long service leave` HAS `the amount paid` IS A NUMBER

-- s 12(6) "The ordinary pay of an employee on long service leave shall be paid to him by the employer when the leave is
-- taken, and shall be paid in one of the following ways, namely: (a) in full when the employee commences his leave; (b) at
-- the same times as it would have been paid if the employee were still on duty; (c) in any other way agreed upon between
-- the employer and the employee". With s 12(4), an employee whose employment ends is "deemed to have commenced to take his
-- leave on the date of the termination", so the pay is due in full that day (fork F10). Day 0 is the day the duty arose
-- (the day the leave was fixed, or the day the employment ended); the employer MUST pay the whole amount WITHIN the days to
-- the day the leave begins. A payment of less than the whole amount does not count.
GIVEN `the amount owed` IS A NUMBER, `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `What is done`
`s 12(4), (6)(a) -- pay the leave in full when it begins` `the amount owed` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `my employer`
    MUST `pay my long service leave` `the amount` PROVIDED `the amount` AT LEAST `the amount owed`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `my employer` BECAUSE "the ordinary pay for the long service leave was not paid in full when the leave began, or on the day the employment ended (s 12(4), (6)(a))"

-- The helper that runs the rule over what happened. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but
-- not BREACH. It takes a DEONTIC value, so it is never exported.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `What is done`
      `the party` IS A `Party`
      `the act` IS A `What is done`
      `the day it was done` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `done in time under` `the rule` `by` `the party` `doing` `the act` `on day` `the day it was done` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day it was done`), (EVENT `the party` `the act` `the day it was done`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Whether I was paid on time` IS ONE OF
    `paid in full on time`
    `paid in full, but late`
    `not yet paid in full, and not yet due`
    `not yet paid in full, and overdue`

GIVEN `the day the duty arose` IS A DATE
      `the day it was due` IS A DATE
      `the amount owed` IS A NUMBER
      `the amount paid` IS A NUMBER
      `the day it was paid` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Whether I was paid on time`
`s 12(4), (6)(a) -- whether my leave pay was paid on time` `the day the duty arose` `the day it was due` `the amount owed` `the amount paid` `the day it was paid` `today` MEANS
    IF `the amount paid` AT LEAST `the amount owed`
    THEN IF `done in time under` (`s 12(4), (6)(a) -- pay the leave in full when it begins` `the amount owed` (`not below zero` (`the days from` `the day the duty arose` `to` `the day it was due`))) `by` `my employer` `doing` (`pay my long service leave` `the amount paid`) `on day` (`not below zero` (`the days from` `the day the duty arose` `to` `the day it was paid`))
         THEN `paid in full on time`
         ELSE `paid in full, but late`
    ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is on or before` `the day it was due`
         THEN `not yet paid in full, and not yet due`
         ELSE `not yet paid in full, and overdue`

@export Was my long service leave pay paid on time, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 12(4), (6)(a))? Where the pay is to be paid in full, it is due when your leave begins; where your employment ends with leave untaken, you are treated as starting the leave that day, so it is due on your last day. (If you agreed to be paid at your usual pay times, or another way, this question does not apply.)
GIVEN `the day the duty arose` IS A DATE @desc The day the dates of your leave were fixed, or the day your employment ended
      `the day it was due` IS A DATE @desc The first day of your leave, or the day your employment ended
      `the amount owed` IS A NUMBER @desc The ordinary pay owed for the whole period of leave, in dollars (see the questions on how much)
      `the amount paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount you have been paid for it, in dollars; 0 if nothing
      `the day it was paid` IS A DATE @desc The day it was paid (any day if nothing has been paid)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Whether I was paid on time`
`Was my leave pay paid on time` MEANS
    `s 12(4), (6)(a) -- whether my leave pay was paid on time` `the day the duty arose` `the day it was due` `the amount owed` `the amount paid` `the day it was paid` `today`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Disputes, recovery and prosecution (ss 13, 14A, 15, 20, 21) -- arises only if there is a dispute or money is unpaid`

-- s 13(1) "If a dispute - (a) as to whether or when - (i) an employee is or has become entitled to long service leave or
-- payment in lieu of such leave; or (ii) a deceased employee's personal representative is or has become entitled to
-- payment in lieu of the employee's long service leave; or (b) with respect to the rate of ordinary pay of an employee for
-- the purposes of this Act - occurs, the employer, employee or personal representative, at his or her discretion, may
-- refer the matter to the Secretary for investigation or apply, under section 29 of the Industrial Relations Act 1984, to
-- the President of the Commission for a hearing by the Commission." (1A)-(4): the Secretary investigates and reports; an
-- unresolved dispute is heard by a Commissioner, who orders payment; s 14: an appeal lies to a Full Bench, whose decision
-- is final. A dispute of another kind: JUST FALSE for s 13 (an "if" condition).
DECLARE `What the dispute is about` IS ONE OF
    `whether or when I am, or became, entitled to long service leave or payment instead of it`
    `whether or when a deceased employee's personal representative is, or became, entitled to payment instead of the leave`
    `the rate of my ordinary pay for long service leave`
    `something else`

GIVEN `the dispute` IS A `What the dispute is about`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 13(1) -- I may refer the dispute` `the dispute` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the dispute`
    WHEN `something else` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@export Can I take my long service leave dispute to the Secretary or the Tasmanian Industrial Commission, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 13)? A dispute about whether or when you (or a deceased employee's personal representative) are entitled to leave or payment instead of it, or about the rate of ordinary pay, may be referred by you, the employer or the personal representative to the Secretary for investigation, or to the Commission for a hearing; a Commissioner's determination can be appealed to a Full Bench.
GIVEN `the dispute` IS A `What the dispute is about` @desc What the dispute is about: whether or when you are entitled to long service leave or payment instead of it; whether or when a deceased employee's personal representative is entitled to payment; the rate of your ordinary pay; or something else
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I refer my dispute` MEANS
    `s 13(1) -- I may refer the dispute` `the dispute`

-- s 14A(1) "A person is not entitled to be represented by a practitioner at the hearing of a dispute under section 13 or
-- the hearing of an appeal under section 14." (2): nothing in (1) prevents an officer or employee of an organization
-- registered under Part V of the Industrial Relations Act 1984 who is a practitioner from representing a person "if he is
-- employed to perform functions that may be performed by persons other than practitioners". (3) "practitioner means an
-- Australian legal practitioner."
GIVEN `the representative is an Australian legal practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the representative is an officer or employee of a registered organisation, employed to do work others may do` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 14A -- I am barred from being represented by this person` `the representative is an Australian legal practitioner` `the representative is an officer or employee of a registered organisation, employed to do work others may do` MEANS
    `yes or no` (    `the representative is an Australian legal practitioner`
                 AND (NOT `the representative is an officer or employee of a registered organisation, employed to do work others may do`))

@export Am I barred from having this person represent me at a long service leave dispute hearing or appeal, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 14A)? You are not entitled to be represented by a lawyer (an Australian legal practitioner) at a hearing under s 13 or an appeal under s 14, unless the lawyer is an officer or employee of a registered employee or employer organisation employed to do work non-lawyers may also do.
GIVEN `the representative is an Australian legal practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who would represent you is an Australian legal practitioner (a lawyer)
      `the representative is an officer or employee of a registered organisation, employed to do work others may do` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that person is an officer or employee of a union or employer organisation registered under Part V of the Industrial Relations Act 1984, employed to do work that non-lawyers may also do
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I barred from this representative` MEANS
    `s 14A -- I am barred from being represented by this person` `the representative is an Australian legal practitioner` `the representative is an officer or employee of a registered organisation, employed to do work others may do`

-- s 15(1) "An employee, or the personal representative of a deceased employee, may apply to a magistrate, as prescribed,
-- for an order directing the employer to pay ... the full amount of any payment that has, at any time during the period of
-- 3 years immediately preceding the date of the application, become due to the employee or to his personal representatives
-- under this Act". (2) the magistrate's determination is final. (3) "where the question of a long service leave
-- entitlement has been determined by a Commissioner under section 13 or by a Full Bench under section 14, payment of that
-- entitlement shall become due on the day of the determination." The 3 years run back from the day before the application
-- to the same day 3 years before it; a payment due on that day, or later up to the application day, is in (fork F11).
-- Outside the period: JUST FALSE (ruling of 2026-11-05 (a)).
GIVEN `the day the payment became due` IS A DATE, `the day of the application` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 15(1) -- I may apply to a magistrate for the payment` `the day the payment became due` `the day of the application` MEANS
    `yes or no` (    (`the day` (`the same day` (0 MINUS 3) `years after` `the day of the application`) `is on or before` `the day the payment became due`)
                 AND (`the day` `the day the payment became due` `is on or before` `the day of the application`))

@export Can I still apply to a magistrate for an order that my employer pay me this long service leave money, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 15)? An employee, or a deceased employee's personal representative, may apply for any payment that became due under the Act in the 3 years before the application; a payment the Commission or a Full Bench determined is due on the day of the determination.
GIVEN `the day the payment became due` IS A DATE @desc The day the payment became due (for leave, the day it should have been paid; on termination, your last day; after a Commissioner's or Full Bench determination, the day of the determination)
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE @desc The day you would apply to the magistrate
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I still apply to a magistrate for this payment` MEANS
    `s 15(1) -- I may apply to a magistrate for the payment` `the day the payment became due` `the day of the application`

GIVEN `the day the payment became due` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 15(1) -- the last day to apply to a magistrate` `the day the payment became due` MEANS
    `the same day` 3 `years after` `the day the payment became due`

@export By when must I apply to a magistrate for an unpaid long service leave payment, under Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 15)? The application must be made while the payment is within the 3 years before the application; this gives the last day.
GIVEN `the day the payment became due` IS A DATE @desc The day the payment became due
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to a magistrate` MEANS
    `s 15(1) -- the last day to apply to a magistrate` `the day the payment became due`

-- s 20(1)(c) "No person shall ... contravene or fail to comply with any provision of this Act that is applicable to him.
-- Penalty: Fine not exceeding 20 penalty units." s 21 "proceedings in respect of an offence against this Act may be
-- commenced at any time within one year after the cause of complaint arises." s 23: a prosecution does not prejudice the
-- employee's rights. The last day is the same day one year on (fork F1).
GIVEN `the day the cause of complaint arose` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 21 -- the last day to prosecute my employer` `the day the cause of complaint arose` MEANS
    `the same day` 1 `years after` `the day the cause of complaint arose`

@export If my employer breaks Tasmania's Long Service Leave Act 1976, by when can it be prosecuted (ss 20, 21, 23)? Proceedings for an offence (for example failing to grant or pay leave, or keeping a false leave record; a fine of up to 20 penalty units) must begin within one year after the cause of complaint arises; a prosecution does not affect your own right to be paid.
GIVEN `the day the cause of complaint arose` IS A DATE @desc The day the contravention happened (for example the day the leave pay was due and not paid)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when can my employer be prosecuted` MEANS
    `s 21 -- the last day to prosecute my employer` `the day the cause of complaint arose`

-- s 20(1) "Penalty: Fine not exceeding 20 penalty units." The penalty unit's value is fixed outside this Act.
`s 20 -- the most an offender can be fined, in penalty units` MEANS 20
